@@ -0,0 +1,110 @@
+---
+node: previews
+---
+
+# Decision: Preview Cache Replacement Policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker holds a private cache of rendered contact sheets, bounded at 12 GiB. A sheet is roughly 2 MiB, so the cache holds on the order of six thousand sheets — a small fraction of an archive in which about 6,000 distinct clips are viewed each week and twenty-four editors work at once.
+
+Editors work in two modes that pull on the cache in opposite directions. A screening session pulls in many one-use sheets, each rendered once and judged on sight. A comparison session pulls in a small working set, revisited repeatedly while an editor weighs one take against another. The cache is sized for the archive, not for either mode, so whenever it is full the replacement policy decides which mode survives.
+
+That decision has a cost the editors feel. A sheet that has been evicted is not merely slow to appear; it costs a render of about 450 ms and a second request that returns a pending ticket. If the policy evicts a comparison sheet in the middle of a long screening, the comparison work degrades into a stream of re-renders. If the policy is too protective, cache space fills with sheets nobody asks for again, and the screening session pays for it.
+
+The replacement policy is entirely internal: the request contract, the twelve-GiB bound, the render queue's concurrency, and the worker's exclusive ownership of cache files and bookkeeping are the same whatever policy is chosen, and no other node observes or depends on it. That makes this a decision about what the worker believes the editors are doing, and about how much work the worker does to believe it.
+
+The mechanics this decision settles are specified in the owning design, `docs/intent/previews/previews-design.md`.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **Scan resistance.** The policy retains a repeatedly reused sheet while a sequential screening of one-off sheets continues long enough to fill the cache. An option that fails this gate is eliminated, not merely scored down.
+- **Bounded carry-over between projects.** Protection is never inherited: a finished project's sheets hold protected space only until the first cache pressure that reaches them, and are never kept alive by a separate decay or aging mechanism. An option that fails this gate is eliminated.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Protection of the reused working set** — *major*, and the criterion the project's tenet "Reuse over recency" turns on: when cached preview space is contended, retain contact sheets editors have returned to before one-off sheets seen once.
+- **Adaptation to a project switch** — *moderate*: how quickly the protected part of the cache follows the editors from one project to the next.
+- **Bookkeeping cost** — *moderate*: work per request and per cached entry, and whether the policy needs background maintenance that competes with rendering on an eight-core worker.
+- **Tuning surface** — *minor*: how many policy parameters must be set well for the policy to behave as intended.
+- **No over-protection** — *minor*: the policy does not reserve space for sheets that were briefly reused and then abandoned.
+- **Predictable occupancy** — *minor*: how much of the budget the protected set may claim, and whether that claim is bounded by the design rather than by accident.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### 2Q: a first-use queue, a hot queue for reused sheets, and a ghost list
+
+**Description.** Every cached sheet starts in a first-use queue, and a request for a sheet already in that queue promotes it to a hot queue. Sheets that have been requested more than once live in the hot queue; sheets requested once live in the first-use queue. A request for a sheet that has never been seen is rendered and enters the first-use queue. Keys of first-use sheets that are evicted are remembered in a ghost list that holds the key and its reference count but no JPEG data, so that a sheet known to have been seen before can re-enter the hot queue directly after a re-render rather than having to earn its way back from the first-use queue.
+
+Reuse is detected by a reference count kept per key: every request for a known key increments it, whether the request is answered with a ready sheet or a pending ticket, whether it shared a render job with other requests, and whether the render later fails. A request for an unknown revision is not a reference, because no sheet can exist for it. The count is read when a sheet is admitted. A sheet whose count has passed one enters the hot queue; a sheet with a count of one enters the first-use queue. A first-use eviction carries the count into the ghost list, and that count is restored to the key when the key is requested again, so the memory of the earlier request survives the eviction; a hot eviction discards it.
+
+Two separate pressures cause eviction. When the two queues together would exceed the cache budget, the least recently used first-use entry is evicted first, and only when the first-use queue is empty does the least recently used hot entry go. When the hot queue alone would exceed its own cap, only hot entries are evicted — screening sheets are not spent to satisfy the cap on the protected set. The hot cap is a configuration value, set for the pilot at 6 GiB, half the budget; the ghost list is capped at 50,000 keys, and the oldest entry is dropped when the list is full. A sheet larger than the hot cap is never admitted to the hot queue, whichever route it arrives by, and stays in the first-use queue; a sheet larger than the whole cache budget is served but never cached, and any ghost entry for its key is kept. A ghost entry is removed when the sheet is admitted to either queue, and survives a failed render.
+
+**Demonstration.** An editor compares six takes of one sequence, coming back to each of them at least once; the six sheets are promoted to the hot queue on their second request. The editor then screens two hundred unrelated clips. Those screening sheets accumulate in the first-use queue and are the first to be evicted, while the comparison sheets stay in the hot queue and are still resident when the editor comes back.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Scan resistance: **passes**. Screening traffic lands in the first-use queue and is spent there; while screening traffic is arriving, the first-use queue is never empty, so the hot queue is not touched.
+- Bounded carry-over between projects: **passes**. Protection is never inherited — a new project's sheets earn their place by being requested — and a previous project's hot sheets are displaced on the first hot-cap breach, with no aging mechanism to wait for. However, they do hold protected space until such pressure arrives; if the new project never fills the hot cap, they keep it.
+- Protection of the reused working set: **strong**. Under budget pressure one-off sheets are evicted first, so a screening cannot reach the reused set. The protection is bounded by the hot cap and by sheet size, not unlimited.
+- Adaptation to a project switch: **strong**. A new project's sheets earn protection by being requested a second time, and a sheet evicted from the hot queue loses its count and returns to the first-use queue.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **partial**. A reference count and a queue transition per request, plus per-key metadata and a ghost list in worker memory; but no periodic aging pass and no background job.
+- Tuning surface: **partial**. Two parameters — the hot cap and the ghost-list size — must be set sensibly.
+- No over-protection: **weak**. A sheet used twice and then abandoned occupies hot space until the hot queue's least-recently-used eviction reaches it.
+- Predictable occupancy: **strong**. The protected set's claim on the budget is capped at a configured share, so it cannot grow by accident.
+
+**Summary.** Protects the reused working set by giving it a queue of its own, at the cost of per-request bookkeeping, a second queue, and a bounded protected set.
+
+### LRU: least recently used
+
+**Description.** Every cached sheet carries the time it was last requested. When the cache is full, the sheet that has gone longest without a request is evicted. Recency is updated whenever a sheet is served, so the cache continuously converges on whatever the editors touched most recently.
+
+**Demonstration.** The same editor compares six takes, screens two hundred clips, and the cache fills during the screening. The comparison sheets are the least recently used, so they are evicted first. The editor's return triggers a fresh render of each take.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Scan resistance: **eliminated**. A sequential screening is precisely the traffic that evicts the working set; the sheets least recently used are the comparison sheets the editor still needs.
+- Bounded carry-over between projects: **passes**. Recency alone carries no history forward, and nothing waits for a decay mechanism.
+- Protection of the reused working set: **weak**. Reuse is indistinguishable from a single recent request.
+- Adaptation to a project switch: **partial**. The cache follows the most recent traffic immediately, but because there is no protected set, there is nothing to carry over — the previous project's working set survives only until the next capacity pressure evicts it.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **strong**. One timestamp update per served sheet; no counters, no background work.
+- Tuning surface: **strong**. No policy parameters.
+- No over-protection: **strong**. A sheet that stops being requested becomes the next eviction.
+- Predictable occupancy: **strong**. There is no protected set to size; the cache holds exactly the budget, and recency — not an unbounded frequent set — decides every eviction.
+
+**Summary.** The cheapest and most adaptive policy, at the price of a screening scan flushing the working set.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+**Description.** Every cached sheet carries a counter of how many times it has been requested. The cache keeps the sheets whose counters rank above a configured threshold and evicts the rest. Because counters would otherwise keep yesterday's favourites alive forever, they are aged: a counter decays, or is periodically halved or dropped, so that interest that has gone away stops counting.
+
+**Demonstration.** An editor compares six takes over the course of a morning, then screens two hundred clips. The comparison sheets hold the highest counters and survive; the screening sheets, requested once, do not. After the editors move to another project, the old counters decay slowly, and the previous project's sheets continue to occupy protected space while the new project's sheets build their own.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Scan resistance: **passes**. One-off sheets never accumulate high counters, so the reused set survives a screening.
+- Bounded carry-over between projects: **eliminated**. The counters are exactly the mechanism that carries a finished project's popularity forward, and the finished project's sheets hold protected space until the aging mechanism drains them.
+- Protection of the reused working set: **partial**. Strong for sheets with genuine repeat traffic; weak for sheets that matter but are requested rarely.
+- Adaptation to a project switch: **weak**. Counters reflect history, so the cache lags a switch by the aging interval.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **partial**. A counter update per request and per-entry counter storage, plus whatever aging work the decay rule requires, which may compete with rendering for the worker's cores.
+- Tuning surface: **partial**. The aging interval and decay rule are policy parameters, as is the counter threshold that separates popular sheets from unpopular ones.
+- No over-protection: **partial**. Abandoned sheets fall out only as their counters age, so they occupy the frequent set for the aging interval.
+- Predictable occupancy: **weak**. The frequent set has no explicit cap; its size follows from the counter threshold and the aging rule.
+
+**Summary.** Keeps the genuinely popular set, but lags a project switch and pays for counters and aging.
+
+## Selection
+
+2Q is chosen.
+
+It is the only option that passes both gates: it survives a screening scan, and its carry-over of a finished project is displaced by ordinary cache pressure rather than by a separate aging mechanism. LRU is eliminated on scan resistance, the property this policy exists to serve; frequency with aging is eliminated on carry-over between projects, which is what makes its counters both useful and dangerous. The gates still have a cost: a previous project's hot sheets hold protected space until pressure reaches them, which 2Q accepts because that space is bounded by the hot cap and shed at the first breach.
+
+On the weighted criteria, the choice turns on the project's tenet "Reuse over recency", which is why protection of the reused working set is the major criterion and adaptation to a project switch is the moderate one. 2Q is strong on both, and it is strong on them in a way LRU cannot be: it distinguishes a sheet an editor has returned to from a sheet an editor has merely seen, which is the distinction the two editing modes turn on. LRU wins decisively on the moderate bookkeeping cost — one timestamp, no background work — and its tuning surface is empty, where 2Q has two parameters to set; both LRU and frequency with aging are strong on not over-protecting abandoned sheets, where 2Q is weak. Those are accepted costs of the major criterion: 2Q buys protection for the reused set with per-request bookkeeping, a second queue, and parameters to tune, and it gives up some abandonment recovery and some guarantee over a working set larger than the hot cap.
+
+The choice also carries costs that are accepted rather than overlooked. A working set larger than the hot cap cannot be retained in full; with the cap at half the budget, a comparison session whose working set exceeds 6 GiB degrades to partial protection. A sheet used twice and then abandoned occupies protected space until recency evicts it. The ghost list consumes worker memory that the JPEG budget does not account for. These bound how far the protection extends, and they are the reason the hot cap is treated as a tuned value rather than a fixed one.
+
+Committing to 2Q obliges the preview worker to maintain per-key reference counts, two queues, and a bounded ghost list, and it obliges the pilot to revisit two parameters against real telemetry: the hot queue cap, which trades protected working-set size against screening sheets, and the ghost-list size, which trades reuse memory against the breadth of remembered evictions. Two further questions stay open for later: whether a hot-queue eviction should leave a ghost entry, and whether reference counts and the ghost list should survive a worker restart. The design currently specifies that a restart re-admits cached files to the first-use queue and starts the ghost list empty; both open questions are recorded in the owning design's deferred items.
+
+The price this project pays for the policy — more bookkeeping per request, and a second queue to tune — is the price of comparison work surviving a long screening.
